  "query_preview": "..A strong high pressure system will remain centered\nover the Arizona/New Mexico border through Friday resulting in\ndaytime temperatures in the mid 80 to lower 90s in the mountains\nto 105 to 110 at elevations below 4000 feet in our forecast area.\nDue to the hot temperatures at lower elevations an Extreme Heat \nWarning is in effect for the lower portions of the Grand Canyon as\nwell as Yavapai county through Friday. Enough moisture will \nremain for the daily development of afternoon into the evening \nthunderstorm, mainly at higher elevations but nothing too \nimpactful is anticipated. \n\nStarting Saturday and lasting into early next week...The high \npressure center will shift eastward in response to a longwave \ntrough and embedded disturbances moving over the western states. \nThe trough will remain just far enough north and west to allow \nmonsoon moisture to stream northward across Arizona. Look for the\ndaily influence of daytime heating on thunderstorm development \nbut with the addition of passing shortwave disturbances \ninteracting with this moisture (classic transition season stuff).\nIts too far out to say much but the pattern will contain a decent\namount of vertical wind shear which could lead to more organized \nthunderstorms with an elevated potential to become severe. In \naddition, about 10-20% percent of model ensemble members are \npointing toward a deeper moisture tap. If the deeper moisture tap \ndevelops cooler trough related temperatures aloft, organized \nvertical wind shear and passing enhancements to upward motion by \nthe weak disturbances could lead to a quite active weekend. Stay\ntuned.",
